      commentary: 'After nine verses of open questioning, the tenth gives a name: Prajāpati, "Lord of Creatures." The unknown god is answered at last — and here honesty requires a note, because it is one of the most revealing facts in Vedic scholarship. The refrain\'s word for "to which" is kasmai, from ka, "who?" In later texts, that very question-word was turned into a proper name: the tradition, unwilling to leave the god of this hymn nameless, personified the question itself and called him "Ka" — the god whose name is Who. And most scholars read verse ten as a later addition, appended precisely to supply the answer the original poet had deliberately withheld. In other words: the hymn was composed as nine verses of magnificent, sustained uncertainty, and a later hand, uncomfortable with a prayer that never names its god, wrote a tenth verse to close the question. Both moves are human and understandable. But notice which one is the greater poem. The power of this hymn lives entirely in the nine verses that do not answer — in the willingness to praise, to hold up the whole cosmos in wonder, and to admit, each time, that the name of its source is not known. That is the same courage the Nasadiya shows in ending on "perhaps even he does not know," and it is why these two hymns of the tenth book are the summit of Vedic thought: they are scripture brave enough to worship a question.'
